Wait — batch insert of evidence rows: since we hold task row lock (FOR UPDATE), concurrent batches on same task serialize. Unique constraint is backstop. If DuplicateKeyException occurs anyway (shouldn't under lock), let it propagate → 500? Better: catch and treat as duplicate → failure. Under row lock it can't happen from another transaction on same task. Leave as is.

received_count increment: for each inserted receipt. For failure path, inserted evidence rows also increment received_count (they are received evidence). Fine.

Note ordering within receiveChunks: task lock first, then read manifest etc. Release cancel: cancel takes release row lock then batch-updates tasks. receiveChunks takes task row lock without release lock — potential deadlock? cancel: UPDATE rollout_task ... WHERE release_id — takes locks on task rows. receiveChunks holds task row lock, doesn't touch release_order row. cancel holds release row lock, wants task row lock. receipt: takes release lock then task lock. receiveChunks: task lock only. No cycle (receiveChunks never waits on release lock). OK.

But wait — receiveChunks doesn't lock release row; cancel may set task CANCELLED concurrently. receiveChunks holds task row lock; cancel's batch UPDATE will wait for the task row lock until receiveChunks commits. After commit, cancel proceeds and cancels. If cancel commits first, receiveChunks' SELECT FOR UPDATE waits, then sees CANCELLED → 409. Consistent commit order ✓.

